<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="exam2">
    <title>Exam 2 - MATH-262-01</title>

    <p>
        <alert>Instructions:</alert> You have 110 minutes to take this exam. Please answer each question in the space provided. Answers without justification may <alert> earn no credit </alert>. You are permitted your signed prep work journal and a calculator on this exam. No other notes or electronic devices of any kind are allowed on this exam. Failure to comply may result in earning a 0 on any portion of this exam and/or being reported to the university. 
    </p>



    <!-- reduction of order --> 
    <exercise>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the ODE <md> 2t^2 u'' + tu' - 3u = 0. </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Show that <m> y_1 = \frac{1}{t} </m> is a solution on <m>(0,\infty)</m>. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use a reduction of order technique to find a second particular solution. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <!-- undetermined coeffs -->
    <exercise workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the method of undetermined coefficients to solve the IVP <md> y'' -4y' - 12y = 3e^{5x}; \ y(0) = \frac{18}{7}, \ y'(0) = -\frac{1}{7}. </md> 
            </p>
        </statement>
        
    </exercise>

    <!-- variation of parameters -->    
    <exercise workspace="7in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use variation of parameters to solve the ODE <md> 2y'' + y' - y = x + 1. </md> 
            </p>
        </statement>

        <hint>
            <p>
                Recall <m>\int xe^{ax} \, \left(\frac{ax - 1}{a^2} \right)dx = e^{ax}  + C</m>. 
            </p>
        </hint>
    </exercise>

    <!-- 2nd-order modelling -->
    <exercise>
        
        <introduction>
            <p>
                According to <alert>Kirchoff's Law</alert>, the impressed voltage <m>E(t)</m> on a circuit is the sum of the potential drops across all components. In an LCR circuit, that is
                <md>
                    V_L + V_R + V_C = E(t).
                </md>
                where <m>V_L</m> is the potential drop across the inductor, <m>V_R</m> is the potential drop across the resistor, and <m>V_C</m> is the potential drop across the capacitor. Suppose the circuit has inductance <m>L</m>, resistance <m>R</m>, and capacitance <m>C</m>. 
            </p>
        </introduction>
        
        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the statements below to write a 2nd-order IVP for the charge on the capacitor <m>q</m> at time <m>t</m>.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The potential drop across a capacitor is the ratio between its charge and capacitance.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Current is the rate of change of the charge on the capacitor. 
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The potnetial drop across a resistor is the product of the resistance and the current.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The potential drop across an inductor is the product of the inductance and the rate of change of the current.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The capacitor starts with no charge or current.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol> 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose there is no impressed voltage (<m>E(t) = 0</m> for all <m>t</m>). Use the ODE you built in part (b) to construct an algebraic model for the charge on the capacitor at time <m>t</m>. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose the capacitor must have a capacitance of 10 pF. Sketch a diagram of the <m>(R,L)</m> parameter space below. Label regions of underdamping, critical damping, and overdamping. Specify the region of this parameter space that your solution falls into. 
                </p>
                <image source="images/blankQ1axes.jpg" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription></shortdescription>
                </image>
            </statement>
            
        </task>

    </exercise>

    <!-- slope fields -->
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose the slope field below corresponds to a first-order ODE.
            </p>
            <image source="images/slope_field_4.png" width="50%">
                <shortdescription>A slope field.</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Identify all equilibria (nullclines) for the system. Sketch them onto the picture above, then write their equation(s) in the space below.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Label at least one nonzero isocline for the system. Sketch it onto the picture above, then write their equation(s) in the space below.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the initial condition <m>y(3) = 1 </m>. Sketch the corresponding trajectory on <m>(0,\infty)</m> onto the picture above.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the initial condition <m>y(-3) = 1 </m>. Sketch the corresponding trajectory on <m>(-\infty,0)</m> onto the picture above.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Classify each of the equilibria you found in part (a) as stable, unstable, or neither on <m>(-\infty, 0)</m>. Justify your answer.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>
